JAVA BASICS - INTERVIEW NOTES
=============================


1. LOOPS
========

Loop = Used to repeat code multiple times.

Types:
1. for loop
2. while loop
3. do-while loop
4. enhanced for loop


FOR LOOP
--------
Use when you know how many times to repeat.

Example:
for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

Output:
0 1 2 3 4

Remember:
for = Fixed/repeated number of times


WHILE LOOP
----------
Use when condition decides how long to run.

Example:
int i = 0;

while(i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}

Remember:
while = Check condition first


DO-WHILE
--------
Runs at least ONE time.

Example:
int i = 0;

do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
} while(i < 5);

Remember:
do-while = Execute first, check later


ENHANCED FOR LOOP
-----------------
Used to easily access elements of array/collection.

Example:
int[] nums = {10, 20, 30};

for(int n : nums) {
    System.out.println(n);
}

Remember:
for-each = Easy way to visit every element



2. ARRAYS
=========

Array = Stores multiple values of the SAME type.

Example:
int[] nums = {10, 20, 30, 40};

Access:
nums[0] -> 10
nums[1] -> 20

Length:
nums.length -> 4

Example:
for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(nums[i]);
}

Remember:
Array = Fixed size + Same type

Important:
Index starts from 0.



3. STRINGS
==========

String = Sequence of characters.

Example:
String name = "Rahul";

Common methods:

name.length()       -> length
name.charAt(0)      -> first character
name.toUpperCase()  -> uppercase
name.toLowerCase()  -> lowercase
name.equals("Rahul") -> compare
name.contains("ah") -> check text
name.substring(1,3) -> part of string

Example:
String s = "Java";

s.length();       // 4
s.charAt(0);      // J
s.toUpperCase();  // JAVA

IMPORTANT:
Use .equals() to compare Strings.

Correct:
s1.equals(s2)

Avoid:
s1 == s2

Remember:
String = Text



4. METHODS
==========

Method = Block of code that performs a specific task.

Example:

static void greet() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}

Call:
greet();

Method with parameters:

static int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int result = add(10, 20);

Remember:
Method = Reusable piece of code

Basic structure:

returnType methodName(parameters) {
    // code
}



5. STRINGBUILDER
================

StringBuilder = Used to modify strings efficiently.

String is immutable.
StringBuilder is mutable.

Example:

StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Java");

sb.append(" Programming");

System.out.println(sb);

Output:
Java Programming

Common methods:

append()  -> Add text
insert()  -> Insert text
delete()  -> Delete text
reverse() -> Reverse text
toString() -> Convert to String

Example:

StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Java");

sb.append("!");
sb.reverse();

Remember:
StringBuilder = Easy + fast string modification

Very useful in:
- String manipulation
- Loops
- Coding problems



6. HASHMAP
==========

HashMap = Stores data in KEY-VALUE pairs.

Example:

HashMap<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put("Apple", 10);
map.put("Banana", 20);

System.out.println(map.get("Apple"));

Output:
10

Common methods:

put(key, value)       -> Add/update
get(key)              -> Get value
remove(key)           -> Remove
containsKey(key)      -> Check key
size()                -> Number of entries

Example:

map.put("A", 100);
map.put("B", 200);

map.get("A");  // 100

Remember:
HashMap = KEY -> VALUE

Example:
Name -> Phone Number



7. HASHSET
==========

HashSet = Stores UNIQUE values.

Duplicates are automatically removed.

Example:

HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add(10);
set.add(20);
set.add(10);

System.out.println(set);

Output:
[10, 20]

Common methods:

add()       -> Add
remove()    -> Remove
contains()  -> Check
size()      -> Number of elements

Remember:
HashSet = UNIQUE values

Important:
HashSet does NOT guarantee insertion order.



8. ARRAYLIST
============

ArrayList = Dynamic/resizable array.

Normal array:
Fixed size

ArrayList:
Can grow/shrink

Example:

ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(30);

System.out.println(list.get(0));

Output:
10

Common methods:

add()       -> Add
get()       -> Get
set()       -> Update
remove()    -> Remove
contains()  -> Check
size()      -> Size

Example:

list.add(10);
list.add(20);

list.set(0, 100);

System.out.println(list);

Output:
[100, 20]

Remember:
ArrayList = Dynamic Array



